  "notes": "The late-90s duo-ballad template, modelled on the ballad band the research names: 85–90 with a half-time feel and the backbeat on 3, so `halfTime` is true and the snare is `halftime_3`. The 104–122 straight uptempo band is the other backbeat class and is named here rather than encoded. Three drum deltas are quoted — \"kick 1 and 3\", \"snare on 3 with a heavy reverb tail\", and \"8th hat opening to 16ths\" — and the sparse fill schedule follows from a programmed kit with a live overdub rather than a fill-driven one. The harmonic colour is add9/sus2 over I–V–vi–IV and vi–IV–I–V, so `susSubstitutionProb` sits at 0.34 and the ninths are authored. The melody claim is the most specific thing in the entry and is encoded directly: \"very stepwise with a leap of a 6th into the chorus apex\", which is a step share of 0.8 with a real `octaveJumpProb` carrying the leap, and \"the ballads stay in one key with a dynamic rather than harmonic climax\" is why no modulation habit is expressed in the arrangement. ⚠ `confidence` is medium because the research rates it high-medium and says the drum detail is inferred. ⚠ The **INFERRED** parameters are all drum probabilities; `bpm.mode` 88 sits inside the narrow published band. ⚠ **`bassline.rhythm` is `boom_chick`, not `reese_sustain`** — the research names the figure outright: *“synth bass on roots in half/whole notes under ballads; 8th-note played bass on the uptempos”*. A held `reese_sustain` writes one note per chord and says the opposite of what the entry describes.",
